WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:12.000
At UST-Legazpi, ultrasonic sensors alone trigger false alarms on pedestrians, while continuous video inference wastes power. ParkSense combines both.

2
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:25.800
Each slot runs a solar ESP8266 node with a TP4056 charger and 3.3-volt regulator. Below 100 cm, it sends a wireless trigger.

3
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:38.500
Over a local Raspberry Pi hotspot—with zero internet needed—an overhead camera checks the slot. If a pedestrian triggered the curb, YOLOv5 rejects it.

4
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:50.500
In the Python Tkinter monitor and mobile app, four color-coded slots update in real time—recording a 15-second detection with YOLOv5 confidence.

5
00:00:51.300 --> 00:01:04.500
Across 80 occupied and vacant trials, ParkSense achieved 100% detection accuracy, 72% average confidence, and a ₱7,544 hardware cost.

6
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:13.500
ParkSense—engineered at UST-Legazpi by Emmanuel Alcazar Junior, Justin Lanuza, and Laurence Nidea.
